## Description

This component represents an LLM-based model-to-model mapping workflow designed to support interoperability between heterogeneous modeling languages and engineering tools in Software Defined Vehicle (SDV) development. The approach combines Large Language Models (LLMs) with Model-Driven Engineering (MDE) techniques to automatically transform model instances according to a given target metamodel or modeling-language definition.

The workflow supports transformations between Ecore and SysML v2 models, including Ecore-to-Ecore, SysML v2-to-SysML v2, Ecore-to-SysML v2, and SysML v2-to-Ecore mappings. Source model instances and corresponding target metamodel definitions are provided as contextual information to the LLM, which identifies semantically corresponding modeling elements and generates a target-compliant model instance while preserving relevant classes, attributes, relationships, containment structures, and values.

To improve reliability, the LLM-based transformation is complemented by an automated validation stage. Generated Ecore/XMI models are checked against their target Ecore metamodels, while generated SysML v2 models are validated against the corresponding SysML definitions. This hybrid LLM/MDE workflow combines the flexibility of generative AI for semantic model mapping with deterministic validation mechanisms for detecting syntactic and structural inconsistencies.

The component is evaluated in automotive and SDV-related modeling scenarios and supports locally deployable LLMs, enabling model transformations to be performed within controlled engineering environments without requiring proprietary cloud-based AI services.

## Rationale

Automotive and SDV engineering workflows rely on heterogeneous modeling languages, metamodels, and engineering tools. Conventional model transformations typically require manually specified transformation rules, such as ATL or QVT mappings, which can require substantial development and maintenance effort when source or target metamodels evolve.

The proposed component uses LLMs to infer semantic correspondences between modeling concepts and generate transformations dynamically. Combining LLM-based mapping with formal model validation reduces the need for manually implemented transformation rules while retaining explicit checks for target-metamodel conformance. The approach therefore aims to improve flexibility, automation, and interoperability across heterogeneous automotive engineering toolchains.

